Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics argues happiness (eudaimonia) is the only good that is always chosen for its own sake — money, health, and friendship are chosen because they lead to happiness, but happiness is chosen for nothing beyond itself.
Positive psychology research (Seligman, Csikszentmihalyi) has operationalised wellbeing through PERMA: Positive emotions, Engagement, Relationships, Meaning, Achievement — components with measurable health and social outcomes.
Cross-cultural surveys (World Happiness Report, Gallup World Poll) show that people in every culture, when asked what they want most for their children, answer some version of 'I want them to be happy' — revealing happiness as a universal terminal value.
